course: dictionary
lesson: 1555
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and change is a good example. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer wants. Ask yourself: what word would fit here?
explain: To change means to make something different. «In a fair test you change only one thing and keep everything else the same.» Here change means alter: one thing becomes different and the rest stays as it was.
explain: Change can also mean money. «If Gran says "keep the change", telling her how much is left makes it an honest gift.» Here change means the coins given back after you pay more than the price.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence. If «keep the change» means "alter the change", it does not make sense, so the money meaning must be right.
fact: Change can be a verb (a doing word): to change is to make something different.
fact: Change can be a noun (a naming word): a change is a time when something becomes different.
fact: Change also means the money you get back when you pay more than the price, or loose coins in your pocket.
fact: «That makes a change» means something is unusual, not the usual way.
fact: «Some words don't change at all: one sheep, two sheep» shows that some words stay the same in their plural form.
exercise: «In a fair test you change only one thing and keep everything else the same.» What does a fair test change? | Nothing, ever | *Only one thing | The whole room | Everything at once
exercise: Gran says "keep the change". What does she mean? | You need a new purse | You must give back the coins | *You can keep the money left over | The shop is closing
exercise: «Darwin's finches have beaks suited to seeds, insects or cactus; over many generations birds whose beaks fitted the food survived better, so beaks changed.» Find and copy one word that means became different. | seeds | insects | finches | *changed
test: Ice melts into water. What kind of change is this? | A change of clothes | A change of plan | *A change of state | Loose change
test: Tom has cereal, not toast, for a change. What does for a change mean? | A train ride | A coin | *Something unusual, not the usual way | Something dirty
exam: A shop item costs 4 pounds and you pay with 10 pounds. How much change is due to you? | 14 pounds | 5 pounds | *6 pounds | 4 pounds
